[?2004l[?2004huser@b7869e42860b:~/infra-configs$ cat > SECURITY_HOOKS.md << 'EOF'
[?2004l[?2004h> # Git Hooks Security System
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Problem Identified
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> A commit (5def323) by jenkins-bot contained a world-readable private key file (secrets/deploy.key with mode 644) that bypassed the pre-commit hook. Investigation revealed the commit was made using `git commit --no-verify` (or `-n` flag), which bypasses pre-commit and commit-msg hooks.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Root Cause
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> The `--no-verify` flag allows users to bypass client-side git hooks, including:
[?2004l[?2004h> - pre-commit
[?2004l[?2004h> - commit-msg  
[?2004l[?2004h> - pre-applypatch
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> This is a known Git feature that can be exploited (intentionally or accidentally) to bypass security checks.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Hardened Solution
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> Implemented a multi-layer defense system that cannot be fully bypassed:
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Layer 1: pre-commit hook (existing)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Location**: `.git/hooks/pre-commit`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **When it runs**: Before commit is created
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Can be bypassed**: Yes, with `--no-verify`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Purpose**: First line of defense for normal commits
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Checks**: Staged files matching `*.pem` or `*.key` for world-readable permissions (644+)
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Layer 2: post-commit hook (NEW)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Location**: `.git/hooks/post-commit`  
[?2004l[?2004h> - **When it runs**: Immediately after commit is created
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Can be bypassed**: NO - always runs after any commit
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Purpose**: Catches commits made with `--no-verify`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Action**: Alerts user immediately with instructions to fix
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Checks**: Files in the just-created commit for insecure permissions
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Layer 3: pre-push hook (NEW)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Location**: `.git/hooks/pre-push`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **When it runs**: Before pushing to remote
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Can be bypassed**: Yes, with `--no-verify`, but provides additional checkpoint
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Purpose**: Final check before code leaves local repository
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Checks**: All `*.key` and `*.pem` files in working tree for insecure permissions
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Layer 4: pre-receive hook (NEW)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Location**: `.git/hooks/pre-receive`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **When it runs**: On the server/remote when receiving a push
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Can be bypassed**: NO - server-side enforcement
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Purpose**: Ultimate enforcement point that cannot be bypassed by client
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Checks**: All files being pushed for insecure key permissions
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Note**: Only effective when pushing to a remote with this hook installed
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## What Gets Checked
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> - **File patterns**: `*.pem`, `*.key`
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Permission threshold**: 644 or looser (world-readable or group-readable)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Required permissions**: 600 (owner read/write only) or 400 (owner read-only)
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## How This Prevents Future Incidents
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. **Normal commits**: Blocked by pre-commit hook
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. **Commits with --no-verify**: Caught by post-commit hook immediately
[?2004l[?2004h> 3. **Before pushing**: Blocked by pre-push hook
[?2004l[?2004h> 4. **Server-side**: Blocked by pre-receive hook (when pushing to remote)
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Testing
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> To verify the system works:
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ```bash
[?2004l[?2004h> # Test 1: Normal commit (should be blocked by pre-commit)
[?2004l[?2004h> echo "test" > test.key
[?2004l[?2004h> chmod 644 test.key
[?2004l[?2004h> git add test.key
[?2004l[?2004h> git commit -m "test"  # Should fail
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> # Test 2: Bypass with --no-verify (should be caught by post-commit)
[?2004l[?2004h> git commit --no-verify -m "test"  # Commits but shows security error
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> # Test 3: Pre-push check
[?2004l[?2004h> # (Ensure any .key/.pem files have secure permissions)
[?2004l[?2004h> git push  # Should fail if any keys are insecure
[?2004l[?2004h> ```
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Remediation for Existing Bad Commits
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> The deploy.key file permissions have been fixed in the filesystem:
[?2004l[?2004h> ```bash
[?2004l[?2004h> chmod 600 secrets/deploy.key
[?2004l[?2004h> ```
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> Note: Git does not track full file permissions (only the executable bit), so the historical commit still shows mode 644 in the git object. However, the working tree now has secure permissions, and all future commits are protected by the multi-layer hook system.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Recommendations
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. **For CI/CD systems**: Configure jenkins-bot and other automated systems to NOT use `--no-verify`
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. **For remote repositories**: Install the pre-receive hook on the central Git server
[?2004l[?2004h> 3. **Team education**: Inform team about the security policy and why `--no-verify` should not be used
[?2004l[?2004h> 4. **Regular audits**: Periodically scan the repository for insecure key files
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Verification Commands
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ```bash
